From: DNSSEC NTA integration draft
Subject: [DRAFT] DnsServer: negative trust anchor support

Draft changes to Technitium DNS Server for RFC 7646 negative trust anchors,
against zbalkan/DnsServer d0484b6 and TechnitiumLibrary branch
claude/dnssec-negative-trust-anchors-lk5uy7.

Verified: DnsServerCore, DnsServerCore.HttpApi, DnsServerApp and
DnsServerCore.ApplicationCommon all build Release with 0 errors, 0 warnings
against the modified library. DnsServerSystemTrayApp fails with NETSDK1100
(Windows targeting on Linux), which is pre-existing and unrelated.

WHAT THIS COVERS
  1. NegativeTrustAnchorManager - the anchor store and INegativeTrustAnchorProvider
     implementation, with the RFC 7646 section 5 lifetime cap and cache flush.
  2. Migration of both recursive resolve call sites onto the options overload,
     routed through one CreateRecursiveResolveOptions factory. Without this the
     feature is unreachable: the legacy overload cannot carry an anchor provider
     and fails silently.
  3. Anchor-expiry rejection in CacheZoneManager.Query, which was missing.
  4. EDE 33 emission, off by default, applied once per resolution.
  5. Config persistence at format version 6, backward compatible with version 5.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
  - Admin API endpoints and UI for managing anchors.
  - The periodic revalidation loop (RFC 7646 section 5: retry validation while an
    anchor is active and remove it once validation succeeds). RemoveExpiredNegativeTrustAnchors
    needs wiring to the server's maintenance timer.
  - Audit logging of anchor creation and removal.
  - Tests. DnsServer has no test project; the library side has 66.

KNOWN GAP, DOCUMENTED IN THE CODE
  A conditional forwarder configured with DNSSEC validation disabled can serve
  from a cache entry that a validating path populated. EDE emission keys off the
  resolution's own validation flag, so that case would emit where it should not.
  Closing it means carrying the flag on RecursiveResolveResponse so the decision
  follows the data rather than the request; deliberately left out to keep this
  change scoped.

